@article{Buzski2015HippocampalSW, title={Hippocampal sharp wave‐ripple: A cognitive biomarker for episodic memory and planning}, author={Gy{\&quot;o}rgy Buzs{\&#x27;a}ki}, journal={Hippocampus}, year={2015}, volume={25}, pages={1073 - 1188}, url={https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:12557226} } G.
Alteration of the physiological mechanisms supporting SPW‐Rs leads to their pathological conversion, “p‐ripples,” which are a marker of epileptogenic tissue and can be observed in rodent models of schizophrenia and Alzheimer's Disease. Sharp wave ripples (SPW‐Rs) represent the most synchronous population pattern in the mammalian brain.
Recording neuron ensembles in the rat medial prefrontal cortex to study memory trace reactivation during SWS following learning and execution of cross-modal strategy shifts found that learning influenced which patterns were most strongly encoded and successively reactivated in the hippocampal/prefrontal network.Expand ... V. Ego-StengelM. Wilson ... It is shown that disrupting neuronal activity during ripple events impairs spatial learning in rats, and interfering with hippocampal processing during sleep led to decreased learning.Expand
@article{Buzski2015HippocampalSW, title={Hippocampal sharp wave‐ripple: A cognitive biomarker for episodic memory and planning}, author={Gy{\"o}rgy Buzs{\'a}ki}, journal={Hippocampus}, year={2015}, volume={25}, pages={1073 - 1188}, url={https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:12557226} } G.
The findings point to the involvement of hippocampal SWRs in establishing and triggering spontaneous recollections in the human brain and highlight the function of SWRs as powerful multitasking signals that contribute both to the encoding and to the spontaneous access and reinstatement of human memories.Expand
